 		<English>[TAB]Traditional education within the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church follows a structured progression through ecclesiastical schools. Students initially enter the Nebab Bet (house of reading), where they acquire literacy skills and commit to memory essential prayers and the Psalms. Subsequent advancement encompasses liturgical studies, chant (zema), and poetry (qene), ultimately leading to the Metsehaf Bet?a specialized institution devoted to advanced textual exegesis.[PARAGRAPH:1]The term Metsehaf Bet functions as an umbrella designation for commentary schools comprising four principal divisions: Old Testament studies (Beluy), New Testament studies (Haddis), patristics and ancillary disciplines (Liqawent, incorporating canon law and computus), and monastic literature (Menekosat). Pedagogical methodology centers on intensive textual analysis, wherein students engage in line-by-line interpretation and examine theological, ethical, and historical dimensions through established commentary traditions.</English>
